A||||9||Jncomutt||3 or 4 quarts??||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||11-08-2001||03:07 PM||||I bet its been covered more than once here, but I found both answers.  So, on my 84 2.5 do I fill it with 3 quarts, 3.5 quarts, or a full 4???||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||11-08-2001||03:38 PM||||Fill it until the oil level is between the Add and Full marks on your dipstick.  Start with 3 quarts and add from there. <P>According to my '88 owners manual, the 2.5 holds 3 quarts of oil.  I don't know if the 84's are identical.  The filter usually adds another 1/2 - 1 quart to that when you change the oil (depending on filter capacity).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DrRodey||11-08-2001||04:33 PM||||The basic rule of thumb i've always heard is a half a quart per cylinder and two in the pan. So for a four cyl=four  six cyl=5 and 8 cyl=6.  It doesn't work for all vehicles but as a general rule it preaty damn good.<P>------------------<BR>Hi... My names Dan, and i'm addicted to Fieros.<BR>'88 Formula CONVERTIBLE<BR>'85 Coupe<BR>'84 coupe T-Top<BR>AOL IM = drrodey<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.JPG">     <BR> <A HREF="http://geocities.com/dr_rodey" TARGET=_blank>WEB SITE</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||neohio||11-08-2001||05:39 PM||||The 84 takes 4 quarts..the owners manual will say 3 but that was a design flaw and eventually turned into one of the recalls because of fires..one thing though..make sure you get the bigger of the 2 oil filters when you change the oil, different manufactures use different size filters, I guess they never changed their catologs after the service bulletins came out..||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||11-08-2001||06:36 PM||||my 84 4 cylinder takes 5 quarts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||11-08-2001||07:29 PM||||My old 88 coupe, and my 84se I have now. They both took like 4.5 to 5 quarts to get up the the full mark on the dipstick. I remember a few other duke owners saying this also.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jncomutt||11-08-2001||09:04 PM||||about those oil filters, does it really make a difference?  I've always used the large filter, today I got the short one instead, thought it was just due to the brand...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||11-08-2001||09:09 PM||||The larger oil filter holds more oil, so you have more oil capacity. That has to be good.  As for capacity - like I said, fill it till it's between Add and Full on the dipstick.  That's the best way to be sure.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||11-08-2001||09:58 PM||||Drop in 3 quarts, check and then add another or whatever is needed.<P>Oil Filters (ACDelco)<BR>PF47 believe is the short one<BR>PF52 is about twice the length<P>Get the PF52!  Which reminds me, my Fiero is due for another oil change.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||11-09-2001||06:03 PM||||All 84's using an OE oil pan get the long PF52. (xxx3980 in the fram numbering system used by many filter makers.) This is per the recall paperwork.<P>Post recall motors also got a new dipstick calibrated to a 4 quart fill. If you think you have a pre recall motor then fill it to 4 quarts, run it to fill the long filter, and scribe a new mark on the stick if needed to indicate where 4 quarts filled it to.<P>All 85-88 4cyl with spin on filters are listed as the short PF47 and a 4 quart fill before and after recall. The long filter is optional on other years. These motors got a new dipstick but it's mainly to move it away from the exhaust.<P>Unlike large body filters, which actually do hold around 1 quart, the narrow body PF47 and PF52 only hold around a pint to a pint and a half at most. The filter media takes up a large chunk of the volume of the shell.<P>A larger filter is a bit misleading.... It does increase the circulation volume of oil, but when the stick says you are low, you are. Don't sit there and think you can let it go below the add mark. <P>I've talked to people who make the filters... In most cases, increasing the filter size doesn't do much unless you are in extremely dirty off road types of situations. If most cars plug their recomended filter before recomended change intervals, somthing major is wrong.<P>The extra oil you gain isn't much either. When you compare a PF47 to a PF52, you gain 1 cup, maybe 1.5 cups at best, of increased oil volume. An extra cup of oil won't hurt but does it really do any good? Maybe a little. It's certainly not like gaining a quart or more like a remote filter/cooler set can.<P>Do I run the long filter in my 87SC? Yes. But mainly because the short one is a Ba$tard to get on and off. Reaching the long filter still isn't fun. 4 quarts of Mobil 1 still puts it between add and full. You can hardly tell on the stick that a larger filter is installed.<P>One other gain you may get is at the higher end of the oil pressure/volume range. The increased surface area might allow more oil flow thru the media before opening the bypass valve. (Very cold oil will likely still bypass the same as with a recomended filter.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
